# VMSTAT(1)

## NAME

vmstat - report system activity

## SYNOPSIS

| **vmstat** [ **-p** ] [ interval [ count ] ]
| **vmstat -s**

## DESCRIPTION

*Vmstat* delves into the system and reports statistics about processes, memory, the two block
devices, the kernel's four rate counters and the distribution of processor time. It reads
everything it prints through kctl(2), which is unprivileged, and opens no device.

The optional *interval* argument causes *vmstat* to report once each *interval* seconds. The
first report is for all time since a reboot and each subsequent report is for the last interval
only. The optional *count* argument restricts the number of reports. With an *interval* and no
*count*, *vmstat* reports until it is stopped. With neither, it prints one report and exits.

The columns, in the order printed:

**procs**
: **r** processes runnable and in core; **b** processes asleep at or below **PZERO**, which is to
  say blocked on a resource rather than waiting for something interruptible; **w** processes
  runnable but swapped out. The swapper is not counted.

**memory**
: **avm** the swappable images of every live process, in words; **fre** the free extents of
  **coremap**, also in words; and, with **-p**, **txt** the percentage of **avm** that is shared
  text in core.

**swap**
: With **-p** only: **i** and **o** are images read from and written to the paging store per
  second.

**disks**
: **md** and **mb** are exchanges completed per second by the disk controller and by the drum.
  Each is a controller and not a drive: *md* covers all the units of **kernel/dev/md.c**.
  *Iostat*(1M) prints three columns per device out of the same histogram, and neither report is
  the other's summary.

**faults**
: **in** device interrupts, **sy** system calls and **cs** context switches, all per second; with
  **-p**, **tr** traps as well.

**cpu**
: The percentage of processor time spent in user mode, in system mode and idle; with **-p**,
  `nice` user mode is separated out as **ni** instead of being folded into **us**.

## OPTIONS

**-p**
: Print the wider report: the four extra columns described above.

**-s**
: Print the counters as they stand, one per line and named, and exit. No interval, no process
  scan and no percentages.

## FILES

None. *Vmstat* reads **proc**, **text**, **coremap**, **dk_time**, **dk_numb**, **dk_wds** and
the twelve rate counters through kctl(2).

## SEE ALSO

kctl(2), iostat(1M), pstat(1M), ps(1)

## BUGS

**Note: the interval is measured in ticks accounted, not in wall clock.** There is no
**boottime** variable on this system and none is wanted: **kernel/clock.c** tallies every timer
interrupt it services into **dk_time**, so the sum of that array over **HZ** is the uptime, and
using it puts every number in a report over the same denominator - the rates and the
percentages cannot then disagree. The interval timer is a flip-flop and free-runs, so a tick
that arrives while delivery is blocked is coalesced; what is measured is therefore a lower
bound on elapsed time. *Iostat*(1M) prints under the same convention.

**Note: the interrupt column counts the clock. HZ** is 250 here, so **in** on an idle machine
is about 250 and device traffic is what moves it above that. That is what 4BSD's **v_intr**
counted too.

**Note: the four percentages are rounded independently** and may sum to 99 or 101. There is no
floating point on this machine and none is wanted for a whole percent.

**Note: -t**, **-i** and **-z** are gone. The first two printed `not applicable` and exited in
the source this was ported from, and are no more applicable here. **-z** zeroed the counters by
writing **/dev/kmem**; kctl(2) reserves **KCTL_SET** and refuses it, and the differential
report is what **-z** was mostly wanted for. **-f** is gone with them: there is no fork
accounting on this system.

**Note: -s** reports no name-lookup statistics. There is no name cache in this kernel, and a
counter nobody keeps is worse than a line nobody prints.

Things can change while *vmstat* is running; the picture it gives is eerily accurate for a
moment that has already passed.
